## Outcome
State the restored behavior and how success is recognized.

## Non-goals
List excluded cleanup, redesign, and unrelated symptoms.

## Requirements Traceability
List each explicit source requirement and point to the reproduction, rule, interface, regression test, or non-goal that addresses it. Do not silently narrow the requested correction.

## Failure and Impact
Describe expected versus actual behavior, affected actors, severity, scope, and environment.

## Reproduction
Give deterministic steps, inputs, preconditions, and observed evidence.

## Root Cause
Explain the violated rule or contract and its owning location. Separate root cause, trigger, symptom, and contributing conditions.

## Regression Test
Identify the test, why its level is reliable, the command, and its expected behavior before and after correction. The CLI records actual results.

## Concrete Examples
Give the failing example plus adjacent happy, edge, and failure cases.

## Key Concepts and Rules
Define relevant terms and the rules the correction must restore. If none, say why.

## Boundaries and Dependencies
Name affected responsibilities, dependency direction, integration points, data ownership, and failure behavior. Follow applicable project instructions for architectural constraints.

## Public Interfaces and Errors
Record compatibility, schemas, commands, APIs, events, and error semantics. If unchanged, say why.

## Applicable Support Envelope
For the corrected path, state the supported inputs and limits, identity semantics, ordering, cancellation, re-registration, shutdown, concurrency, and scale guarantees that apply. Mark irrelevant dimensions as not applicable; do not broaden the fix for unsupported hypothetical behavior.

## Correction and Extension Pressure
Describe the correction at the rule-owning location. List affected abstractions and contracts, and record related extension pressure without generic layering.

## Existing Capabilities and Reuse
Identify the closest existing behavior, utility, abstraction, or pattern inspected. State whether the correction extends it; if it adds parallel code, name the concrete semantic or ownership difference that prevents reuse. A superficial name or shape match is not sufficient.

## Test Traceability
Map root cause, neighboring risks, rules, and boundaries to regression, unit, and integration tests. Explain any gap.

## File and Module Placement Plan
List expected new, modified, moved, or deleted files/modules and each responsibility, applicable `AGENTS.md` constraint, boundary, and slice. Paths are reviewed forecasts, not an exhaustive manifest: correction may touch support files or adjust paths when responsibilities, module constraints, and dependency direction remain intact. Use directories or globs when exact paths are premature.

## Implementation Plan
Use `### Slice N: <observable outcome>` subsections. Every slice must include `Outcome`, `Entry point`, `Core behavior`, `Boundary integration`, `Tests`, `Acceptance command`, and `Complete when`. `Acceptance command` is a JSON string array that runs the corrected observable path. Keep the regression lock-in observable and make correction slices runnable across every affected layer.

## Implementation Conformance
Complete during build. Record non-material placement deviations with rationale; restart design review for material architecture or plan deviations.

